# Renown (playtest 2, X3; L22: renown was hard to raise): twice a year a chance to be noticed is pushed
# (`renown_chance`), and a handful of cards in the pools stake renown on a hard challenge: much to gain, a real
# price to fail. Some open only once you're known.

@event renown_chance
title: A Chance to Be Noticed
trigger: auto
when: term and (month == 2 or month == 8) and not final_year
repeat: 6
priority: 25
type: menu
class: routine
tone: tense
art: trophy
tags: renown
scene: A noticeboard in the entrance hall crowded with announcements, one gold-bordered notice pinned dead centre, students jostling to read it.
text: {A gold-bordered notice goes up in the entrance hall. Twice a year the academy puts students on show.|The gold notice is up again, as it is twice a year, and the crowd around it is bigger.|The gold notice again. People glance at you as they read it now.} Three stages: a spell before the whole school, the great debate, or the village fair. Each is a hard test in public. {Win, and the school knows your name; lose, and it remembers that instead.|The village is invited, and the faculty sit in the front row: a win is talked about for months, and so is a loss.|There's a prize, and there's the shame of losing in public.}
> demonstrate: Cast a spell before the school
  hint: Arcana, hard. Fame or embarrassment.
  queue renown_demonstration now
  say: You write your name on the list before you can think better of it.
> debate: Enter the great debate
  hint: Lore, hard. The faculty will be listening.
  queue renown_debate now
  say: You sign up for the debate. Your hands are shaking already.
> fair: Perform at the village fair
  hint: Charm and a spell. The village pays and remembers.
  queue renown_village_fair now
  say: You put your name down for the village fair. Somebody will have to teach you to bow.
> pass: Keep your head down
  mood +tiny
  say: {You read the notice and walk on. Fame can find someone else this term.|Not this time. You've got enough to do without an audience.|People glance at you as you walk past the notice. Let them. You'd rather not lose in public this term.}

@event renown_demonstration
title: Before the Whole School
trigger: queued
class: routine
tone: tense
art: wand
tags: renown magic
scene: The Great Hall packed to the rafters, every house at its table, a single student standing in the middle of the floor with a wand raised.
text: The whole school is in the Great Hall, and so is the faculty, and so, at the end, is the headmistress. You're in the middle of the floor, and everyone will remember what happens next, good or bad. There's a long silence while they wait for you to begin.
> left: Cast the hardest thing you know
  challenge spell adept drift mid:
    win:
      renown +large
      arcana +small
      say: It works, beautifully, better than ever before. The hall is silent and then very loud. For a month, first-years point at you in corridors.
    lose:
      renown -small
      mood -medium
      say: It fizzles in front of everyone, with a noise like a sad goose. The hall is very kind about it, which is unbearable. You'll be "the goose" until someone else fails.
> right: Cast something safe, perfectly
  renown +small
  mood -tiny
  say: Something simple, done flawlessly. Polite applause. Nobody will remember it, and nobody will laugh at you either.

@event renown_debate
title: The Great Debate
trigger: queued
class: routine
tone: tense
art: scroll
tags: renown study
scene: Two lecterns on a stage under banners, a student at one pointing emphatically, an older student at the other frowning, a packed hall of faces in the dark.
text: The great debate: "That magic should be taught to anyone who asks." You've drawn the side you disagree with, against a fourth-year who has never lost. The faculty are in the front row, and Thorne is taking notes: win and they'll remember you, lose and so will everyone.
> left: Argue your side brilliantly anyway
  challenge research expert drift mid:
    win:
      renown +large
      lore +small
      rel thorne +small
      say: You argue the side you don't believe so well that you half-believe it. The fourth-year concedes. Thorne, afterwards, says "Hm," twice, which has never happened.
    lose:
      renown -small
      mood -small
      say: The fourth-year takes you apart, politely and completely. The hall applauds you for trying, which is the cruellest kind of applause.
> right: Argue what you actually believe
  renown +tiny
  charm +small
  mood -small
  say: You throw away the brief and argue your own side, which is against the rules. You lose on points and win the hall. Thorne writes something down, and you'll never know what.

@event renown_village_fair
title: Magic at the Fair
trigger: queued
class: routine
tone: light
art: sparkle
tags: renown money
scene: A small stage at a village fair under bunting, a student conjuring coloured lights over a crowd of apprentices and farmers.
text: The village fair has a stage, bunting and a crowd of farmers who have seen every trick there is. The village apprentices sit at the front, cross-legged, unimpressed in advance. Win them over and the village talks of you for a year; flop, and it talks of that.
> left: Put on a real show
  challenge negotiate journeyman:
    win:
      renown +medium
      gold +small
      say: Lights, a dancing hat, a dove that turns into a shower of petals. The apprentices whoop. The farmers pay for the hat trick twice. The village talks about it until harvest.
    lose:
      renown -tiny
      mood -small
      say: The dove refuses to turn into anything and sits on your head. The apprentices love it; the farmers don't pay.
> right: Do card tricks for the front row
  renown +small
  mood +small
  say: You sit on the edge of the stage and do card tricks for the apprentices until your voice gives out. No fame to speak of. Half of them walk you to the gate.

@event renown_ice_rescue
title: A First-Year on the Ice
class: incident
tone: tense
when: season == "winter" and term
weight: 5
art: snow
tags: renown danger
scene: A small figure clinging to the edge of a broken patch of ice in the middle of a frozen lake, students shouting on the shore, a student running out across the ice.
text: A first-year has gone through the ice on the lake. A crowd is shouting on the shore, and nobody is moving. You're nearest.
> left: Go out on the ice
  alignment +tiny
  challenge feat adept:
    win:
      renown +large
      health -small
      say: You crawl out flat on the ice and pull them free. By supper the whole school knows your name, and the first-year's mother writes you a letter you keep for ever.
    lose:
      health -large
      renown +small
      say: The ice gives under you too. The prefects pull you both out with a ladder. You spend a week in the infirmary, a little famous, mostly frozen.
> right: Run for the prefects
  renown +tiny
  mood -small
  say: You run for help, and the prefects get there with ladders in time. Someone else is the hero. You were right, and you feel wrong about it all winter.

@event renown_portrait
title: A Portrait for the Hall
class: routine
tone: light
when: renown >= 30
weight: 8
art: mirror
tags: renown money
scene: A painter's easel in a sunlit corridor, a half-finished portrait of a student in formal robes, the sitter trying very hard not to scratch their nose.
text: The academy hangs a portrait of its most talked-about students in the long gallery each year, and someone has suggested you. The painter's fee is yours to pay, as is the custom. The other portraits are already watching you.
> left: Sit for it
  gold -medium
  renown +medium
  mood -tiny
  say: Six sittings, an itchy collar and a painter who hums. The portrait is good, if a little more heroic than you are. Passing it, first-years straighten their backs.
> right: Decline politely
  mood +tiny
  say: You decline. Your purse is grateful. The portrait that goes up instead is of someone who looks very pleased with themselves, and you find you don't mind at all.

@event renown_rumour
title: A Rumour About You
class: incident
tone: tense
when: renown >= 40
weight: 5
art: mask
tags: renown social
scene: Two students whispering behind their hands in a corridor, a third walking past them with a stiff back, a crumpled gossip sheet on the floor.
text: Being known has a price: there's a rumour about you going round the castle, untrue and a little bit funny, and it's in Nessa's gossip column by Friday. People stop talking when you walk in.
> left: Answer it in public
  challenge negotiate journeyman unlikely wild:
    win:
      renown +small
      say: You laugh it off at supper, loudly and well, with a better story of your own. By Monday everyone's repeating yours instead.
    lose:
      renown -small
      mood -small
      say: You deny it too hard, and it sounds true. The rumour grows a second chapter. It'll pass. It takes a month.
> right: Ignore it
  renown -tiny
  mood -tiny
  say: You say nothing. It burns for a fortnight and then dies of boredom. You learn who repeated it, and remember.

@event renown_patron_dinner
title: Dinner With a Patron
class: routine
tone: tense
when: renown >= 25 and term
weight: 8
art: meal
tags: renown money
scene: A long candlelit dining table in a grand townhouse, a student in borrowed formal robes seated between a wealthy patron and a portrait of the patron.
text: A wealthy patron of the academy has asked to meet "the students everyone's talking about", and you're one of them. Dinner is nine courses. The patron likes clever talk and has been known to fund a promising student's whole final year.
> left: Be dazzling
  challenge negotiate expert:
    win:
      renown +medium
      gold +medium
      say: You're witty through eight courses and wise on the ninth. The patron sends you home with a purse "for books" and an invitation to come back next year.
    lose:
      renown -small
      mood -small
      say: You use the wrong fork, then the wrong joke. The patron is kind and bored. You're not asked back.
> right: Be honest about your struggles
  renown +small
  gold +small
  say: You talk plainly about money and work and exams. The patron listens, the only one at the table who does. A small purse arrives the next week with no letter, just a crest.
